    question: "A customer asks you a technical question and you do not know the answer. How would you handle it?",
    slug: "a-customer-asks-you-a-technical-question-and-you-do-not-know-the-answer-how-would-you-handle-it-pre-sales",
    shortDescription: "[COMMON] Fresher / — Pre-Sales Fundamentals & Communication. Focus on customer needs, technical credibility, business value, and a clear next step.",
    sampleAnswer: "I would not guess. I would acknowledge the question, explain what I know if useful, and state that I want to verify the exact answer before committing. I would record the question, consult the appropriate product or engineering resource, and follow up within an agreed timeframe. This protects technical credibility because an accurate delayed answer is better than a confident incorrect answer.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source classification: [COMMON]\nExperience level: Fresher /\n\nAnswer:\nI would not guess. I would acknowledge the question, explain what I know if useful, and state that I want to verify the exact answer before committing. I would record the question, consult the appropriate product or engineering resource, and follow up within an agreed timeframe. This protects technical credibility because an accurate delayed answer is better than a confident incorrect answer.\n\nExample:\nExample response: ‘I don’t want to guess on that behavior. Let me verify it with our product documentation/engineering team and come back with the exact answer by tomorrow.’\n\nInterview Tip:\nUse a structured response: understand the customer/problem first, explain your reasoning, connect the technical solution to business value, and state what you would do next. Avoid making assumptions or promises that you cannot support.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nCustomer communication, discovery, listening, empathy, business-value thinking, technical credibility, and ability to adapt communication to the audience.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n• Start with the customer’s objective, not a feature list. • Ask clarifying questions before recommending a solution. • Separate facts, assumptions, risks, and commitments. • Never invent a technical answer when you are uncertain. • Connect technical capabilities to measurable customer value. • Document important decisions, gaps, owners, and next steps.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n• What would you do if the customer disagreed? • How would you handle a technical limitation or risk? • How would you measure whether your approach worked? • What would you communicate to the Account Executive or engineering team?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n• Talking about product features before understanding the customer’s problem. • Guessing when the technical answer is unknown. • Overpromising roadmap items or unsupported functionality. • Treating every prospect request as equally important. • Giving a generic sales answer without technical reasoning or customer context.",
    explanation: "This is a [COMMON] pre-sales interview question for Fresher /. The source emphasizes: Use a structured response: understand the customer/problem first, explain your reasoning, connect the technical solution to business value, and state what you would do next. Avoid making assumptions or promises that you cannot support.",
    keyPoints: "• Start with the customer’s objective, not a feature list. • Ask clarifying questions before recommending a solution. • Separate facts, assumptions, risks, and commitments. • Never invent a technical answer when you are uncertain. • Connect technical capabilities to measurable customer value. • Document important decisions, gaps, owners, and next steps.",
