    detailedAnswer: "Source classification: [COMMON]\nExperience level: Intermediate / Pre-Sales Engineer / Consultant\n\nAnswer:\nBuild the demo around 2–4 customer-relevant scenarios. Map each scenario to a business problem, demonstrate only the capabilities needed to prove value, and leave secondary features for discussion. Validate the flow beforehand and keep backup screenshots or a recorded path for critical failures.\n\nExample:\nExample: For a security platform, instead of showing every feature, demonstrate a realistic incident: detect an event, investigate it, apply a response, and show the resulting business impact.\n\nInterview Tip:\nUse a structured response: understand the customer/problem first, explain your reasoning, connect the technical solution to business value, and state what you would do next. Avoid making assumptions or promises that you cannot support.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nTechnical judgment, solution design, feasibility assessment, risk management, cross-functional collaboration, and ability to avoid unsupported commitments.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n• Start with the customer’s objective, not a feature list. • Ask clarifying questions before recommending a solution. • Separate facts, assumptions, risks, and commitments. • Never invent a technical answer when you are uncertain. • Connect technical capabilities to measurable customer value. • Document important decisions, gaps, owners, and next steps.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n• What would you do if the customer disagreed? • How would you handle a technical limitation or risk? • How would you measure whether your approach worked? • What would you communicate to the Account Executive or engineering team?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n• Talking about product features before understanding the customer’s problem. • Guessing when the technical answer is unknown. • Overpromising roadmap items or unsupported functionality. • Treating every prospect request as equally important. • Giving a generic sales answer without technical reasoning or customer context.",
